PLACES=[
N('constable-hall-constableville','Constable Hall','Constableville','mohw','landmark','standing',43.56173,-75.42178,'exact','open','drive','','1810–1819',
  'A Federal limestone manor house built between 1810 and 1819 for William Constable Jr., son of the William Constable who opened great tracts of the North Country to settlement. The museum says the hand-cut stone was probably quarried at Talcottville and hauled in by ox cart, and that local artisans, outside craftsmen and Oneida people built it. The family lived here until 1948; the house was then bought, restored and handed to the Constable Hall Association, and opened as a house museum on July 19, 1949. Wikipedia repeats a local story that Clement Clarke Moore, a cousin of the builder\'s wife, had this house in mind in "A Visit from St. Nicholas"; it is a story, not a fact.',
  'A two-story limestone house with two one-story wings, a two-story pedimented portico with paired slender Doric columns, and a U-shaped court behind formed by the frame servants\' quarters and the carriage house. The museum says the house keeps much of its original furniture, its plan and detail unchanged, and much of the Constable library and papers. Inside by guided tour (about an hour); a garden and the grounds are open free all year.',
  'A North Country manor house that never lost its furniture.',
  'Run by the Constable Hall Association, 5909 John Street (County Route 50) at NY 26. Tours $12 adults, $10 seniors, veterans and students, under 13 free (the museum\'s Plan Your Visit page, read November 20, 2026); stop at the TOUR sign and the guide shows you where to park beside the hall. Phone 315-397-2323. Grounds free all year.',
  [W('Constable Hall'),L('Constable Hall: Plan Your Visit','https://constablehall.org/visit'),L('Constable Hall: the Hall','https://constablehall.org/about/hall/'),HM('Constable Hall',152677),HL],
  brick='no',hours='Thursday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., Sunday noon to 4 p.m., last tour 3 p.m., until September 13; by reservation after that. Grounds open all year'),
N('collins-house-cemetery-constableville','Jonathan C. Collins House and Cemetery','Constableville','mohw','landmark','standing',43.56861,-75.42056,'area','view','drive','cemeteries','1797–1802',
  'One of the first houses of the Constableville settlement, built between 1797 and 1802 for Jonathan C. Collins: a five-bay, center-entry frame house on a stone foundation with a large rear wing. Across the road is the Collins family burying ground, with burials from 1793 to 1943 and several fine stones carved from local stone. Listed on the National Register in 1988. A Collins married into the Talcott family of Talcottville (Town of Leyden history).',
  'The 1790s farmhouse from the road, and the small family cemetery opposite it with its early local-stone markers. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private house on West Road (Register address); look from the road. Whether the family cemetery may be visited was not found in any source; ask first. Pin from the Register list, not checked against an address.',
  [W('Jonathan C. Collins House and Cemetery'),L('Town of Leyden: Edmund Wilson and his Talcottville relations (PDF)','https://townofleydenny.gov/application/files/4817/5298/1683/Leyden_History.pdf')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('constableville-village-historic-district','Constableville Village Historic District','Constableville','mohw','town','standing',43.56347,-75.43043,'area','public','walk','','about 1828–1900',
  'The old village round the Sugar River, listed on the National Register in 1983 with 105 contributing buildings: shops, houses, public buildings and churches built mostly between about 1828 and 1900, a late-1800s rural cemetery, one historic bridge and an early-1900s school. The district runs roughly between the Sugar River and Main, North Main, West Main, Church, High, West and James streets.',
  'A walk along Main and West Main streets for the 19th-century storefronts, churches and houses, the old cemetery and the historic bridge over the Sugar River. Constable Hall is 700 m east on John Street. Which buildings survive since 1983 was not checked; interiors not checked.',
  '',
  'Public village streets (Village of Constableville); the buildings are private, so look from the sidewalks. A mapped public lot is on West Main Street.',
  [W('Constableville Village Historic District'),L('historypointer: district boundaries','https://historypointer.com/nris/resource/83001703')],
  th=[43.56258,-75.43023],thn='Lot on West Main Street',brick='?'),
N('collinsville-cemetery-west-turin','Collinsville Cemetery','West Turin','mohw','burial','standing',43.62648,-75.39422,'site','public','drive','cemeteries','1810',
  'A country burying ground begun in 1810 by the first New England settlers in the Lyons Falls district; about 380 graves, often grouped by family. In 1917 or 1918 an association was formed that turned it into a lawn cemetery: it levelled the ground, reset and straightened the stones, built the east wall and hired a caretaker. Listed on the National Register in 2014.',
  'A short dry-stone wall along East Road with limestone steps carved "1884" in the middle gap; big maples over the stones; some brush round the older graves. No formal paths.',
  '',
  'Owned by Collinsville Cemetery, Inc., an active cemetery at 4061 East Road (the address puts it in Turin). Visit by day and keep to the grass. Park on the road shoulder.',
  [W('Collinsville Cemetery (New York)')],
  brick='?'),
N('gould-mansion-lyons-falls','Gould Mansion Complex','Lyons Falls','mohw','landmark','standing',43.61806,-75.36056,'site','view','walk','','1902',
  'The house of Gordias H. P. Gould, who opened the Gould paper mill at the meeting of the Black and Moose rivers in 1895; he built it in 1902 of Gouverneur marble, across from the mill, under the architect of Forest Presbyterian Church. The complex listed in 1978 is the house, the carriage house and barn, and the office building that was the Gould Paper Company\'s headquarters. The mill passed through several owners, closed in 2000 or 2001 (sources give both), and more than twenty of its buildings have been demolished; its site on Center Street is now vacant land owned by the county\'s development corporation, with a hydro plant.',
  'The marble house and its outbuildings. The carriage house, owned by the village, holds the Lyons Falls Library at 3918 High Street. The cleared mill site lies across the road toward the falls. Interiors not checked.',
  'A paper baron\'s marble house facing the empty ground where his mill stood.',
  'Who owns the house itself was not found; look from High Street and Center Street. The library in the carriage house is public: the village page gives no hours and sends you to the library (phone 315-348-6180).',
  [W('Gould Mansion Complex'),L('Lyons Falls History Association: Gould Paper Company','https://lyonsfallshistory.org/post.php?pid=7'),L('Village of Lyons Falls: library','https://lyonsfallsny.gov/library/'),BOA],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('forest-presbyterian-church-lyons-falls','Forest Presbyterian Church','Lyons Falls','mohw','worship','standing',43.61942,-75.36328,'site','view','walk','','1894',
  'The "Forest Church" congregation was formed in 1826 and in 1854 built a small wooden church in a pine grove below the falls, where the Black and Moose rivers meet. By the 1890s the village had grown on the other side of the river, so a new church was built in its centre to designs by Albert W. Fuller of Albany; the last service in the old church was held on February 4, 1894 and the new one opened on February 25, 1894. The old church was taken down and its organ, carpet, bell and windows were reused in the new one. Listed on the National Register in 2004.',
  'A brown-stained shingle-style church with Gothic touches, a square bell tower with flared eaves and a porte-cochère; beside it the 1902 Foursquare manse and a 19th-century barn. The bell and windows came from the 1854 church (Pomeroy Foundation marker text). A Pomeroy marker for the first church stands at Laura Street and Lyons Falls Road. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Active Presbyterian church at 4109 Center Street (village church list). No page of its own was found with service times (a site of the same name is a church in Forest, Virginia); look from the street.',
  [W('Forest Presbyterian Church'),L('Pomeroy Foundation: Forest Church marker','https://www.wgpfoundation.org/historic-markers/forest-church'),L('Village of Lyons Falls: churches','https://lyonsfallsny.gov/churches/')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('wildwood-cemetery-fisher-chapel-lyons-falls','Wildwood Cemetery and Mary Lyon Fisher Memorial Chapel','Lyons Falls','mohw','burial','standing',43.60833,-75.34528,'site','view','drive','cemeteries','1906; chapel 1921',
  'A cemetery opened in 1906 on River Road, with 736 marked burials, and a Late Gothic Revival chapel built in 1921 and dedicated in 1922 in memory of Mary Lyon Fisher, daughter of Lyman R. Lyon, for whose family Lyons Falls is named. Utica architects Gouge & Ames designed it. Listed on the National Register in 2011.',
  'The chapel of blue limestone with 20-inch walls, a white marble tablet over the door, maple floors and six windows of amber glass; a trap door in the floor hides a lift that took coffins to a basement vault where up to 15 were kept through the winter (Lyons Falls History Association). A plaque to Caleb Lyon Sr. stands in the cemetery. Whether the chapel is opened was not found.',
  'A cemetery chapel with a winter vault for coffins under its floor.',
  'Active cemetery on River Road. Who owns and runs it, and when the chapel opens, were not found: look from River Road unless the cemetery posts that visitors are welcome.',
  [W('Wildwood Cemetery and Mary Lyon Fisher Memorial Chapel'),LFH],
  brick='no'),
N('black-river-canal-locks-lyons-falls','Black River Canal Locks 103–109, Lyons Falls','Lyons Falls','mohw','canal','ruins',43.62028,-75.36167,'site','view','walk','canal','1850s–1922',
  'Lyons Falls was the northern end of the Black River Canal proper, 109 locks in 35 miles from Rome, finished to here in the 1850s (the History Association says 1855). From the turning basin a last flight of locks took boats round the falls and down to the Black River, which was navigable north to Carthage. The American Canal Society numbers them 103 to 109; 108 and 109, a double lock with a 12-foot lift each, made the final drop to the river. In the 1940s part of the canal was filled and piped to make room for the paper mill.',
  'The county\'s 2019 report counts four locks in the abandoned canal north of the turning basin, their walls patched over the years, and recommends signs. The village\'s Canal Basin Park on the turning basin has an interpretive kiosk, benches and a picnic table. Concrete supports of the 1916 three-way bridge still show in the Black River (History Association).',
  'The flight of locks where the Black River Canal stepped down round the falls.',
  'Canal Basin Park belongs to the Village of Lyons Falls (county report). Who owns the canal bed and lock walls north of the basin was not found: look from the park and the streets. Parking: the mapped lot at Community Memorial Park, 700 m southeast; Canal Basin Park is on the turning basin itself.',
  [ACS,BOA,L('Lyons Falls History Association: Black River Canal','https://lyonsfallshistory.org/post.php?pid=18'),L('Lyons Falls History Association: three way bridge','https://lyonsfallshistory.org/post.php?pid=5'),W('Black River Canal')],
  th=[43.61419,-75.35708],thn='Community Memorial Park lot',conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('black-river-canal-lock-95-port-leyden','Black River Canal lock by Port Leyden Community Park','Port Leyden','mohw','canal','ruins',43.58735,-75.34894,'exact','view','walk','canal','1850s–1922',
  'A stone lock of the Black River Canal just north of Port Leyden, beside NY 12. The Port Leyden Bicentennial Committee put up a marker here in 1996 calling it Lock 96; the American Canal Society\'s index puts Lock 95 on this spot ("just north of Port Leyden by community park") and Lock 96 about 600 m to the northwest. The canal reached Port Leyden in 1850 (Leyden schoolhouse nomination).',
  'The lock beside the community park and the 1996 marker. The canal society lists more locks near the village: Lock 94 along Canal Street about 1.8 km south, and Locks 99 to 101 with a guard lock about 1.7 km north. What state the walls are in was not described by any source opened.',
  '',
  'HMdb says the lock and marker are next to Port Leyden Community Park, at or near 3205 NY 12; the park\'s owner, hours and parking were not checked (the geocoded address lands on a business lot, so no parking point is given).',
  [HM('Black River Canal Lock 96',258685),ACS,W('Black River Canal')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('black-river-canal-locks-87-90-lyonsdale','Black River Canal flight of four, Locks 87–90','Lyonsdale','mohw','canal','ruins',43.53931,-75.32637,'site','view','drive','canal','1850s–1922',
  'North of the Moose River the canal climbed toward Port Leyden through a flight of four locks, numbered 87 to 90 in the American Canal Society\'s index, which marks all four as seen in 2011. Locks 80 to 84, between the Boonville combines and the Moose River, were destroyed when an early version of NY 12 was built.',
  'Four lock chambers in a row, which show plainly on the bare-ground LiDAR in the grass median between the two carriageways of NY 12 (the index puts them within about 100 m), with Lock 91 a little to the north. Condition not described by any source opened.',
  '',
  'In the median of divided NY 12, a fast highway with no shoulder parking found: do not stop on the highway or cross the lanes on foot. Ownership not checked (presumably highway land, not confirmed). Best seen on the LiDAR or the aerial.',
  [ACS,W('Black River Canal')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('st-marks-church-port-leyden','St. Mark\'s Church','Port Leyden','mohw','worship','standing',43.58111,-75.34972,'site','view','walk','','1865',
  'A small Gothic Revival Episcopal church of 1865, board-and-batten frame, three bays by five, at West Main and Elm streets. Listed on the National Register in 1998 as one of the historic churches of the Episcopal Diocese of Central New York.',
  'The board-and-batten Carpenter Gothic church from the street. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'At West Main and Elm streets (Register address). Whether it still holds services was not found; look from the street.',
  [W("St. Mark's Church (Port Leyden, New York)"),L('historypointer: address','https://historypointer.com/nris/resource/98001003')],
  brick='no'),
N('stone-house-talcottville','The Stone House (Edmund Wilson House)','Talcottville','mohw','landmark','standing',43.53471,-75.36575,'area','view','drive','','1789–1793',
  'Hezekiah Talcott, a Tory in the Revolution, bought 1,600 acres here after the war, and with his sons built a mill, a log house and then this stone Georgian house, begun in 1789 and built over four years. Thomas Baker kept a hotel in it and quarried gray limestone nearby. About 1920 the father of the writer Edmund Wilson bought it for his wife, a Baker granddaughter; Wilson summered here and wrote of it in "Upstate". In 1972 his daughter sold it to a family from Virginia, the first owners outside the Talcottville relations (Town of Leyden history). Listed on the National Register in 1973.',
  'A limestone house, three bays wide and four deep. Interior not checked.',
  'A 1790s stone house that a famous critic made the subject of a book.',
  'Private house off Talcottville Road, south of Port Leyden (Register address); look from the road. Pin from the Register list, not checked against an address.',
  [W('Edmund Wilson House'),L('Town of Leyden: Edmund Wilson and his Talcottville relations (PDF)','https://townofleydenny.gov/application/files/4817/5298/1683/Leyden_History.pdf')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('leyden-common-school-2-talcottville','Leyden Common School No. 2 (Leyden Town Hall)','Talcottville','mohw','civic','standing',43.53383,-75.36724,'exact','view','drive','','1870',
  'A one-and-a-half-story frame schoolhouse built in 1870, now the Leyden Town Hall. Its Register form describes four gable ends, the north front with separate doors and vestibules for girls and boys. The classroom running east to west through the middle is now the town meeting room. Listed on the National Register in 2016.',
  'Clapboards to the ground, a standing-seam metal roof (wood shingle in a 1910 postcard), the paired front doors, and a few original six-over-six windows; inside, the classroom has beadboard walls under a 1986 dropped ceiling, which hides the top panes of the windows (Register form).',
  '',
  'Town of Leyden building, 6606 School Road, Talcottville, with an unpaved lot round it (Register form). Office hours not checked.',
  [L('National Register form (2016)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/16000392_text')],
  brick='no'),
N('martinsburg-town-hall-first-courthouse','Martinsburg Town Hall (first Lewis County Courthouse)','Martinsburg','mohw','civic','standing',43.73722,-75.46889,'site','view','drive','','1812',
  'Built in 1812 as the Lewis County Courthouse and called the oldest public building in the county. Martinsburg was the county seat until 1864, when the railroad was laid on lower ground near the river and the seat moved to Lowville (Town of Martinsburg). In 1870 the building was remodelled in the Italianate style as the Martin Institute, with a pedimented front pavilion and a Palladian window; in the 1890s it became the town hall. Listed on the National Register in 2001.',
  'A clapboarded Federal building with its 1870 front pavilion and Palladian window, on NY 26. The Lewis County Historical Society says the Martinsburg Historical Society is housed in it; the town\'s page puts the society\'s office in the clerk\'s office four lots south. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Town of Martinsburg, NY 26 (Main Street) in the hamlet. Look from the road; town office hours not checked.',
  [W('Martinsburg Town Hall'),L('Town of Martinsburg: our history','https://martinsburgny.gov/our-history'),HL],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('first-lewis-county-clerks-office-martinsburg','First Lewis County Clerk\'s Office','Martinsburg','mohw','civic','standing',43.73657,-75.46884,'exact','open','drive','','1847',
  'A one-room Greek Revival office built in 1847 for the county clerk, of brick in Flemish bond on a limestone foundation, four lots south of the 1812 courthouse. After the county seat went to Lowville in 1864 it served other uses; from 1920 to 1964 the water commission and fire department had it and cut a garage door into the front. The Martinsburg Historical Society leased it from the town in 1983 and restored it over five years, rebuilding part of the front; it has been a local museum since. A 2014 Pomeroy marker in front calls it the "site of" the clerk\'s building, but the building stands. Listed on the National Register in 2017.',
  'The small brick office with its strong cornice returns, an early-1800s local door with a new transom, two original windows on the north side, and inside a paneled ceiling with reproduction lamps, one cast from a fixture in the old courthouse. Used for museum displays (Register form). The HMdb photograph of the marker shows red brick walls with stone lintels and sills, and a sign for the office and the Martinsburg Historical Society.',
  'A county seat that moved away, and the one-room office it left behind.',
  'Owned by the Town of Martinsburg and used by the Martinsburg Historical Society as its office and museum, 6660 NY 26. The town\'s page names "Martinsburg Day", the second Saturday of August, as the society\'s open day; other hours were not found.',
  [L('National Register form (2017)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/100001769_text'),L('Town of Martinsburg: our history','https://martinsburgny.gov/our-history'),HM("Site of First Lewis County Clerk's Building",152595)],
  brick='yes',hours='Martinsburg Day, the second Saturday of August; other days not found'),
N('walter-martin-house-martinsburg','General Walter Martin House (Greystone Manor)','Martinsburg','mohw','landmark','standing',43.73778,-75.46917,'site','view','drive','','1803–1805',
  'The house of General Walter Martin (1766–1834), founder of the town of Martinsburg and a leader in its industry, churches and schools. The stone Federal main block, two stories under a hipped roof with a pedimented centre pavilion, went up between 1803 and 1805 (the 1952 state marker) or in 1805 (Wikipedia); Greek Revival wings were added about 1835. Listed on the National Register in 2008.',
  'The stone house with its centre pavilion and pediment, and the 1952 marker at NY 26 and Whittaker Road. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private house on NY 26 at the crest of the hill in Martinsburg (Register address 6575 Main Street; the marker gives 6675 NY 26); look from the road.',
  [W('Gen. Walter Martin House'),HM('Greystone Manor',82411)],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('west-martinsburg-methodist-church','Methodist Episcopal Church of West Martinsburg','West Martinsburg','mohw','worship','standing',43.75972,-75.51667,'area','view','drive','','about 1840',
  'A country Methodist church of about 1840 on West Martinsburg Road: a clapboarded frame rectangle under a plain gable roof, with a two-stage bell tower and steeple. Listed on the National Register in 1983.',
  'The clapboarded church and its steeple from the road. Whether it is still used was not found; interior not checked.',
  '',
  'On West Martinsburg Road (Register address). Look from the road. Pin from the Register list, not checked against an address.',
  [W('Methodist Episcopal Church of West Martinsburg')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('pine-grove-community-church-watson','Pine Grove Community Church','Pine Grove','mohw','worship','standing',43.75213,-75.3768,'exact','view','drive','','1895–1896',
  'A union church raised in 1895–96 at the Pine Grove crossroads, by its charter open to any religious group. Lucian Rumble, a local caretaker, built it and acted as its architect, and he copied the inside from a little theatre he had once seen. The organ came from the Lowville Baptists, a bell was bought in 1905, and electric lights came in 1937. A preservation society was formed to look after it, and it was listed on the National Register in 2009.',
  'A two-story frame church with a two-story bell tower in the middle of its front. Inside, the seats stand on a sloping floor like a theatre, with the back legs of the chairs cut short, so latecomers had to walk up from the front in full view (NYGenWeb, from the preservation society). A Pomeroy Register marker stands at the church. Whether the inside is ever opened was not found.',
  'A crossroads church built with a theatre\'s sloping floor.',
  'Pine Grove Community Church Preservation Society, 6853 Austin Road (Glenfield address), Town of Watson. Look from the road.',
  [W('Pine Grove Community Church'),L('NYGenWeb: Pine Grove Community Church','https://www.nygenweb.net/lewis/Churches/Watson/pinegrovechurch.html'),L('Pomeroy Foundation: Pine Grove Community Church marker','https://www.wgpfoundation.org/historic-markers/pine-grove-community-church/')],
  brick='no'),
]
